# ansible-lint configuration for deploy/ansible
#
# Why this file exists
# --------------------
# CI installs ansible-lint unpinned (see .github/workflows/ci.yml → ansible-validate).
# ansible-lint 26.x recursively discovers the entire roles/ + playbooks/ tree
# (older releases effectively linted a single file, which is why past `main` runs
# were trivially green). The version is now pinned in CI for reproducibility, and
# this file makes the enforced rule set explicit rather than "whatever the latest
# release defaults to".
#
# Profile
# -------
# We enforce the `min` profile: the floor of correctness/safety rules that must
# always hold (syntax-check, no-log-password, internal-error, etc.). All genuine
# defects surfaced by ansible-lint 26.6.0 lived in this tier and have been fixed
# (invalid play-level `always:` in restore.yml / test-restore.yml, an orphaned
# `include_role` in roles/backup, and a stray `{% endif %}` in service-discovery).
#
# The stricter `basic`-tier findings that remain are style/convention preferences
# (role-name underscores vs hyphens across 28 roles, yaml formatting, command-vs-shell
# idiom, register-var prefixes, no-changed-when on read-only commands). Converging on
# those — especially renaming every role, which ripples through every playbook
# reference — is a deliberate cleanup epic, not a CI-unblock, and is intentionally
# out of scope here. Raise the profile to `basic`/`production` when that work lands.
#
# Known remaining (non-blocking) items to fold into that cleanup epic:
#   - vars.example.yml defines llamenos_postgres_enabled / llamenos_caddy_enabled
#     twice (lines ~49/50 vs ~300/303). The second pair was meant to be distinct
#     maintenance/monitoring toggles; dedup requires renaming the keys AND wiring
#     the new names into the consuming roles, so it is left for that epic.
profile: min
